A directive modifies an existing construct. Template-head directives modify
their template. Source directives attach to the next declaration or statement
when their own contract allows it.

### Two locations

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    message #= [$md: Hello from Moth]

    $export $doc [: Public greeting.]
    greeting #= "Hello"

    $todo [: Add a better error message.]
    assert(false, "Not implemented")
]

`$export` publishes declarations or direct-selection dependency clauses from
`@mod.moth` and `@package.moth`. `$config` marks an explicitly typed
top-level compile-time binding as a build input.

`$doc` documents a declaration. `$todo` annotates a declaration or
statement. Both take a compile-time `String` value, often written as a
template. `$doc` records declaration documentation. `$todo` has no runtime
or builder effect. Use [$html:<code>$doc &#91;$md: ...&#93;</code>] to request Markdown explicitly.

### Stacking and operands

Source directives may share one line or use consecutive lines. No commas
separate them. Each distinct directive attaches independently to the same
following-line construct and order never changes its meaning. Any repeated
directive name on one target is an error.

Comment-only lines can intervene, but a blank line breaks attachment. Another
source directive ends a preceding operand only at the outer expression
boundary, not inside a nested template. A directive cannot skip an invalid
target.

Directives use `$name`, `$name value` or `$name key = value`, according
to their contract. Parenthesized calls like `$name(...)` are invalid.
`$export` and `$config` take no operand.

Template-head directives include `$before`, `$after`, `$fresh`,
`$slot`, `$in`, `$note`, `$raw`, `$md`, `$html`, `$css`,
`$escape_html` and `$code`. Template heads use commas between items.
Only `$note` discards a comment template.

See @../directives (Directives), @../templates/#template-directives (Template
directives) and @../project-structure/#build-inputs (Build inputs).
